<% content_for :title, "About Kevin Skilton" %>

<section class="hero-panel">
  <div class="hero-main">
    <p class="section-kicker">About Kevin</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title">A builder who'd rather verify something works than trust that it sounds like it does.</h1>
    <p class="hero-copy">I'm a Rails engineer who spends a lot of time on how software actually behaves under real use, not just how it reads in a diff. Outside of work, that same instinct shows up in $10 hardware projects that take a lot longer than they should.</p>
    <div class="hero-badges">
      <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/kskilt" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="badge badge-cyan">LinkedIn</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/kskilt" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="badge badge-slate">GitHub</a>
      <span class="badge badge-fuchsia">Rails · Ruby · product engineering</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <%= render "shared/built_with", technologies: [ "Rails 8", "Tailwind" ] %>
</section>

<section class="content-panel">
  <div class="content-stack">
    <p class="section-kicker">My story</p>
    <h2 class="panel-title">How I got here</h2>
    <p class="panel-copy">
      I have a B.A. in English Literature, not a CS degree — I got into this by way of an employer-run coding bootcamp in 2019, and never left. Since then I've gone from junior developer to Software Engineer II, spent a couple of years establishing how our Rails codebase tests itself, and now build the AI-integration side of the product: model selection, retry and fallback handling, conversation logging — the kind of work where "looks right" and "actually works" are very different bars to clear.
    </p>
    <p class="panel-copy">
      Away from a keyboard, I'm a dad to a toddler and owned by two cats. I'm a fairly serious nerd — D&amp;D, Star Wars, Magic: The Gathering, Arduino and Raspberry Pi projects — and the kind of person who'll spend a weekend turning two broken Game Boys into one working Game Boy Pocket with a color screen. I also yard-sale and thrift-shop on the side, mostly hunting for old tech worth bringing back to life.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="content-panel">
  <div class="content-stack">
    <p class="section-kicker">What I've learned</p>
    <h2 class="panel-title">A few things I keep relearning</h2>
    <p class="panel-copy">
      Most of this came out of a weekend spent debugging a touchscreen that turned out to be the wrong hardware entirely<% if @debugging_post %> — <%= link_to "the whole story is on the blog", post_path(@debugging_post) %>.<% else %>.<% end %>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-stack">
    <p class="card-kicker">Verify, don't trust confidence</p>
    <p class="panel-copy">An AI tool can sound completely certain while being completely wrong. The confidence isn't signal — the only way to know a fix works is to check it against the real thing, not how sure the tool sounds.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-stack">
    <p class="card-kicker">Respect what you're building on top of</p>
    <p class="panel-copy">When you're contributing to someone else's project, match how they already do things. Showing up with more work for a maintainer to clean up isn't collaboration.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-stack">
    <p class="card-kicker">The debugging is the job</p>
    <p class="panel-copy">The fix is rarely the hard part. Reading the actual chip markings, testing one variable at a time, re-checking your own assumptions before blaming the tool — that's the real work, and it's still usually a human's job to do.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="content-panel">
  <div class="content-stack">
    <p class="section-kicker">Currently</p>
    <h2 class="panel-title">What I'm focused on</h2>
  </div>

  <ul class="marker-list">
    <li>Building and running this site myself — Rails 8 on Fly.io, deployed with Kamal, with a small agent-assisted workflow for locating, implementing, and validating changes before they ship.</li>
    <li>Running <%= link_to "my CYD-Klipper fork", "https://github.com/kskilt/CYD-Klipper-3.5Resistive", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %> daily on my 3D printer, after the debugging weekend that convinced me AI confidence and correctness are two very different things.</li>
    <li>Building the AI-integration surface at Within3 — a multiturn chatbot with model selection, retry/fallback handling, and conversation logging, from the database schema up.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="content-panel">
  <div class="content-stack">
    <p class="section-kicker">Get in touch</p>
    <h2 class="panel-title">Find me</h2>
    <p class="panel-copy">
      <%= link_to "LinkedIn", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/kskilt", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %> ·
      <%= link_to "GitHub", "https://github.com/kskilt", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %> ·
      <%= link_to "the blog", blog_path %>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
